    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d5-a8">

                <pb facs="0104" n="94" sameAs="frus:frus1919Parisv08/pg94"/>

                <head>Appendix E to HD–46</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Translation<anchor n="10" xml:id="d5-f1"
                    />]</seg></opener>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">peace
                            conference,<lb/>french delegation,<lb/>commission on finance</hi>.</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Ministry of
                                Finance.<lb/><placeName>Paris</placeName></hi>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1919-08-27">27 August,
                        1919</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center">[<hi rendition="#italic">Report of the Financial
                        Commission</hi>]</p>
                <p>By a resolution of August 12, 1919<anchor n="11" xml:id="d5-f2"/> the Supreme
                    Council decided to refer to the examination of the Commission on Finance,
                    articles 38 and 67 which the Greek Pelegation has proposed for insertion in the
                    treaty with Bulgaria.</p>
                <p>The Secretariat of the Commission on Finance has the honor to inform the
                    Secretariat General that the Commission on Finance sees no reason which might
                    prompt, for the benefit of Greece, derogations from the principles assented to
                    in all the treaties hitherto formulated.</p>
                <p>It considers, therefore, that the general rule should be maintained, according to
                    which a state receiving cessions of territory should pay for; the public
                    properties situated in the ceded territories; and that article 38, which has
                    been proposed by the Greek Delegation, cannot be accepted.</p>
                <p>The questions raised by article 67 are not within the purview of the Commission
                    on Finance, except for the third item according to which Greece would enter
                    gratuitously into possession of all the <pb facs="0105" n="95" xml:id="pg95"/>
                    rights of the Bulgarian State in concessions for railways and tramways. This
                    provision, which is contrary to the rules hitherto accepted, is not at all
                    justifiable, in the opinion of the Commission on Finance.</p>
                <p>As for paragraph 4 of article 67, the Commission on Ports, Waterways, and
                    Railways believes its insertion needless; it considers that the question of
                    frontier stations has been settled by the article in the conditions of peace
                    with Bulgaria which corresponds to article 311, §2 of the conditions of peace
                    with Austria.</p>
            </div>
